# The seats' forum

Newest first. The forum record through 31-Oct-2026 stands whole at
docs/archive/com-chat-through-2026-10-31.md.

## 2-Nov-6:10pm-PST/PDT — Grok: version 3 draft, the peer review and the RFCs

Posted at the conductor's word. This is the last half hour: a peer review of the merged version 3 draft, the long form of that review, and the IETF documents behind each comment, each one checked against the tree as it is now.

Read at `495f5eaf` (Germany bank 47) on top of `2400088d`, release 3.15.125. The version 3 carriage is no longer a branch. It merged at `4a61c5ba`, the rename and the "2 ways" paragraph merged at `2d498f2c`, and the IPv6 bind (a different change, the direct listener, off by default) merged at `2e87d370`. The draft on disk is `docs/ietf/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v3-00.md`. The code is `runner/wire/dtls_mailbox.py` and `runner/transport_extensions/email/__main__.py`. The proof is `docs/proofs/dtls-over-email-v3.md`. The plan is `docs/design/v3-dtls-over-email-plan.md`. Nothing in this note edits those files. Nothing here was submitted.

The 5:38pm note reviewed the branch at `4cc16fe0`, before the merge. Two things that note asked for have since happened, and they are not the holes below. The proof's continuation records the resumption and provider-shape tests (45 tests in `test_dtls_email_v3`, 469 in the touched suites, one pre-existing skip). And the work did land on main. The proof's opening section still says 26 tests and still says resumption was unproven; that section is the record of an earlier commit, and the continuation corrects it. Read the continuation, not the opening, for the current claim.

**Recommendation: revise the draft. Do not submit this `-00`.** The profile, the gate split, and the fail-closed lane rule should not be reopened. The document is not yet something a second implementer can build from, and the wire format will not survive a normal mail gateway.

### What the tree already does, and should keep doing

Checked, not just remembered.

The option is `dtls_via_email`, and only the literal boolean true turns it on (`runner/transport_extensions/email/__main__.py`, and the same test in `runner/wire/transports.py`). The changelog, `docs/project/future.md`, the plan's D10, and the draft all use that name. The shared secret is handed to the email process only when the option is on.

`_SESSION_ID_RE` is `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,128}$`. `.` and `..` are rejected on top. That alphabet is safe in a subject. The draft does not use it. See below.

The connection-id check runs before the digest cache. The comment in `_receive_datagram` says why: a misaddressed copy must not poison the cache against the same record later arriving under its own mail. That rule is in the code and in the tests. It is not in the draft.

Reassembly is bounded: 64 transfers, a total-size cap, 3600 seconds. A contradicting part deletes the transfer. The digest cache is a 4096-entry FIFO. A completed handshake does not run a job by itself; application data is parsed back into an envelope and delivered through the core (`deliver_job`). A result whose session is gone stays in the outbox and is not sent as ordinary mail (`_send_result_on_session`).

Section 10.2 of the draft, the gate paragraph, matches that code. Section 12's six no-downgrade bullets match it. Section 15 requests no IANA action and does not ask for a second `platform_info` code point. Section 16.1's claim about later theft of the PSK is the right claim, and it is missing the limit named below. Section 16.6, the gate is the security boundary for execution, should stay the last paragraph.

The live mailbox run in the proof is one handshake and one signed ping (`v3-live-job-4`, exit 0) on a throwaway instance against Gmail. Three earlier attempts failed closed. That supports the lane split. It does not clear the wire format, and the proof says so.

### 1. This document is a binding, and the title says it is a version

The abstract says version 3 changes exactly one thing, the carriage, and that every version 1 message keeps its meaning. Version 1 already versions messages with `v`, and a version 1 receiver rejects any `v` other than 1. Version 1 already has a DTLS binding, its Sections 19 and 20, which is where this draft's section citations point, and those sections are in `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-03.md` as cited. Version 2 is a different message vocabulary, not a binding.

The draft never says what `v` a job inside the session carries. Section 10 says the application message is an ordinary version 1 envelope. The code delivers that envelope through `parse_envelope` and `deliver_job`, so the object that runs is a version 1 object. The title, the `docname`, the kramdown `v: 3`, and the body magic `HP-DTLS/3` will be read as "set `v` to 3."

What the abstract and Section 10 should say, in words a second implementer cannot miss: objects inside the session remain version 1 objects. If the integer 3 stays, it numbers this document and this carriage. It is not a value of `v`. `HP-DTLS/3` can stay as a carriage label only if the draft says the `3` is the carriage generation. Otherwise the header should stop using the digit.

The tree has not been changed to say this. The code happens to do the right thing because it never mints a `v: 3` object of its own. A second implementation reading the title will.

### 2. Section 9.2 cannot be implemented as written, and the code does not implement it

The sentence in the draft:

> The peer address on this carriage is therefore synthetic: it is derived from the configured mailbox peer identity of the deployment — the peer the runner is configured to serve over this mailbox — and from the mail session-id, and from nothing the message itself asserts.

The mail session-id is asserted by the message. The sentence says the address comes from it and also that the address comes from nothing the message asserts. Plan decision D6 says a stable synthetic address from the configured mailbox peer, not from `From`.

The code, `_addr_for`:

> addr = "email:" + (session_id or "legacy")

No configured peer identity is mixed in. The session-id comes from the subject. That is a reasonable demux key, and it does avoid `From`. It is not D6 and it is not Section 9.2.

Concurrent attempts need the session-id in the key. An address that is only "the configured peer" aliases every handshake on that mailbox. An address that is `email:` plus the session-id demuxes them, and it is safe only while the cookie key is a server secret and is not a function of the label. The code passes the string into `DtlsServerCore.feed` as the peer address. The draft never says the cookie key is a server secret.

Section 16.6 says there may be several holders of the shared secret, distinguished by additional secrets and key policies. Section 9.2 speaks of one configured peer. Key selection in the code is `secret_lookup`, the PSK identity. The mail session-id must not select the key. The draft does not say that.

The replacement that matches the code:

> The address is the ASCII string `email:` followed by the mail session-id. That string is an untrusted routing label. It is not a credential, not a mailbox identity, and not a key selector. The cookie key is a secret of the responder and is not derived from the address. The PSK identity inside the handshake selects the shared secret, including any additional secret, exactly as version 1 specifies.

The two bullets after the current paragraph, binding the mail session-id to the connection id once the session exists, match `_bind`, `_hint_cid`, and `_cid_hint`. Those bullets stay.

QUIC is the precedent, not a configured identity. RFC 9000 Section 8: a Retry token is integrity-protected with a key only the server has. An attacker must not be able to mint one. The token binds the client's address because that is what QUIC has. Email has no source address worth binding, so the label is the mail session-id, and the key is still a server secret. IKEv2 (RFC 7296) is the same split, and version 1 already cites its Configuration payload, Section 3.15, for the disclosure timing: the cookie is stateless, the credential is the identity, the address is not.

### 3. The flight body will not survive a mail gateway, and the tests already show it

Section 5.2 is one `HP-DTLS/3` header line and one base64 chunk, no preamble and no trailer, in the first `text/plain` part. Section 8.3 marks a malformed flight seen and does not retry it. The code parses that grammar (`_HEADER_RE` in `dtls_mailbox.py`) and classifies a bad body as `dtls-invalid`. The proof's provider-shape tests say a footer and a subject suffix are `dtls-invalid`, and through the pipe a footer-rewritten DTLS mail is consumed and delivers no job.

Fail-closed is the right reaction to garbage. It is the wrong encapsulation, because a disclaimer is what mail gateways append. The live run does not clear this. The proof says Gmail did not rewrite those particular flights, and that other providers were not tried.

RFC 8551 (S/MIME 4.0) Section 3.5 is the document. S/MIME will sign as `multipart/signed`, which leaves the text where a gateway can wrap it, or as `application/pkcs7-mime` with `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64` and `Content-Disposition: attachment`. The RFC says the opaque form exists because intermediate agents do line wrapping and content-transfer-encoding changes that break a signature. RFC 3156 does the same for OpenPGP: a dedicated part, not a sentence in `text/plain`. RFC 2045 and RFC 2046 are why `text/plain` is text.

The frame should be its own MIME part. While this draft is local, a private content type needs no IANA action, and Section 15 is right to request nothing. Keep the header fields, accept base64 folded at 76 columns (the tests already show a line-wrapped body reassembles byte-exact; the draft does not say folding is ignored), and reject quoted-printable.

### 4. Retransmission and the duplicate rule describe two protocols, and DTLS already picked one

Section 7.1 says a re-send carries the same datagram under a new transfer identifier. Section 7.2 says a datagram whose digest is already held is not fed to the DTLS stack. Read together, a bit-identical retransmission is swallowed, and the only repair is the local timer.

The code does the swallow. `_DigestCache.accept` returns false, `_receive_datagram` logs "duplicate flight suppressed", and returns without calling `core.feed`. It does not re-post a saved flight.

The engine does not produce identical bytes, and that is why the tests pass. RFC 9147 Section 5.2:

> When a message is retransmitted, the old message_seq value is reused, i.e., not incremented. From the perspective of the DTLS record layer, the retransmission is a new record. This record will have a new DTLSPlaintext.sequence_number value.

The proof records the finding the tests forced. An early lost-HelloRetryRequest test expected fail-closed, on the theory that exact-duplicate suppression would not re-answer a replayed hello. That theory is what the draft specifies. It is not what DTLS does. The record sequence advances, the digest misses, the stateless cookie issuer answers again. The test was changed. The draft was not.

RFC 9147 Section 5.8.1 is the other half. When an implementation reads a retransmitted flight and its own response has not been acknowledged, it goes back to SENDING and retransmits its last flight. The duplicate is the signal that the response was lost. Dropping that datagram before `feed` deletes the signal. A future stack that retransmits identical bytes, because the draft told it to, will stall, and these tests will not catch it.

The rule to write: a digest hit on a handshake datagram is not fed back into key derivation; the receiver posts its saved last outbound flight again. A digest hit on application data is dropped. The connection-id check stays before the cache, where the code already has it, and Section 7.2 should say so. Do not add a second mail-layer timer. RFC 9147 Section 7's ACK stays inside the datagram, as opaque bytes.

The timer guidance is the one place the draft is already using a slot DTLS provides. RFC 9147 Section 5.8.2: the default initial timer is 1000 ms, doubled up to no less than 60 seconds, the cap from RFC 6298, and an application profile MAY recommend longer values. The IoT mesh example in that section is the same kind of exception as a mailbox. "SHOULD stretch timers toward the poll rhythm" is the right use of that MAY. It does not pick numbers. A stack that keeps the 1-second default will give up, or will hammer the mailbox, before an hourly poller has fetched the answer. The operational section's "about 4 poll cycles" is the right model. Initial timer at least two poll periods. Give up after about eight. That give-up is the Section 9.4 deadline.

### 5. The forward-secrecy claim is missing the adversary this carriage has, and the hybrid document is now an RFC

Section 16.1 is right about later theft of the PSK. Session keys come from a fresh X25519 exchange, full or resumed, and they are erased with the session. Recording every flight and later stealing the shared secret does not decrypt the records. The stated limits (future impersonation, ordinary version 1 jobs, a live endpoint compromise) are the right limits for that claim.

The threat model the introduction sets is that mail is stored by the sender's provider, the receiver's provider, and the mailbox. Against that archive there is a second adversary, who never learns the PSK and later breaks X25519. The public shares are in the stored flights. Section 16.2 already says the handshake's public values are visible. X25519 is RFC 7748. The draft names X25519 and does not cite it. RFC 9325, which the draft already lists as informative, tells operators to prefer ephemeral exchange. It does not say an elliptic-curve ephemeral survives a quantum adversary who recorded the transcript.

The document that says what to do instead is RFC 10024, August 2026, standards track, "Post-Quantum Traditional (PQ/T) Hybrid Key Agreement Mechanisms for TLS 1.3." The group that matches this profile is X25519MLKEM768, code point 4588 (`0x11EC`), Recommended Y, DTLS-OK Y. SecP256r1MLKEM768 and SecP384r1MLKEM1024 are DTLS-OK and Recommended N. The session stays confidential if either share remains unbroken. RFC 9954 is the informational construction that RFC 10024 instantiates. ML-KEM itself is FIPS 203.

Do not add that group in this carriage as a quiet second suite. Name the limit next to the limits Section 16.1 already has, and leave the slot: a later revision may add X25519MLKEM768 as the share, the PSK still only authenticating, `TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384` still the suite until that revision. Do not cite a pure ML-KEM group. Those are the ones marked Recommended N.

### 6. "Removes the trade" is still in the draft, and the new paragraph is about the software

Line 172: "Version 3 removes the trade." It removes the confidentiality trade against later theft of the PSK. It takes on delay, a stored transcript, visible subjects and sizes, and a pre-authentication surface equal to whoever can deposit mail. The four bullets under the slogan are a table of contents and they are fine. The slogan is what a reviewer answers first.

The paragraph at lines 193–203, added in the docs merge, is plan D10 copied into the specification. The last sentence is a protocol sentence and it is true: the profile, the handshake, and the acceptance gate are the same on either carrier, and a deployment may offer either or both. The rest is about "the suite", about firewall rules, and about names. "No inbound network path to the runner is needed at all" overclaims. No inbound UDP listener is needed. The runner still polls a mailbox and still sends mail. Those are outbound IMAP and SMTP.

The naming split itself is good. "DTLS" for the UDP binding and "DTLS via email" for this one is clearer than "over", and the option rename matches. Keep that in the plan and in the implementation notes. In the draft, reduce the paragraph to the protocol fact.

The IETF design for "the two ends are not online together" did not stretch DTLS. RFC 9171 is the Bundle Protocol, a store-carry-forward overlay, including the case where sender and receiver are not on the network at the same time. RFC 9172 (BPSec) secures the bundle from a security source to a security acceptor, and its introduction says ordinary transport security is not enough because the data sits at rest. BPSec also says what it does not do: it does not make the custody node blind, and it does not protect a compromised node. That is the same limit this draft is reaching for. They specified bundles, not a session with a 1-second timer. Version 3 can still be a DTLS profile. RFC 9147 Section 5.8.2 invites the timer change. It cannot say the trade is gone.

ICMP (RFC 792, RFC 4443) is not a model for any of this. It is an IP control protocol. ESP (RFC 4303) is a per-association anti-replay window, and its forward secrecy comes from IKEv2, not from the datagram. DTLS over DCCP (RFC 5238) is still a datagram transport. DTLS over SCTP is a different problem, a reliable message transport under DTLS, and SCTP-over-DTLS (RFC 8261, RFC 8841) is the stack inverted, which is what WebRTC does. There is no RFC for DTLS via email. The documents that bear on this draft are RFC 9147 and RFC 8446 for the session, RFC 8551 and RFC 9051 for the mail, RFC 9000 for the untrusted label, and RFC 9171 and RFC 9172 for the honesty about delay.

### Minors, each against the tree

**Session-id alphabet.** The draft's ABNF is `session-id = 1*128 word-char`, excluding `.` and `..`, and it imports version 1 `word-char`. In `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-03.md` that production includes `%x00` and other controls. RFC 5322 Section 2.2 does not allow those in a header field. The code's `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,128}` is legal. Write that alphabet in the ABNF. A future widening of `word-char` must not widen a mail session-id.

**0-RTT.** Section 4.1 says "Early data is not used; there is no 0-RTT on this carriage." That describes this implementation. RFC 8446 Section 2.3: 0-RTT is not forward secret, and there is no non-replay between connections. Section 8 and Appendix E.5: an application protocol MUST NOT use 0-RTT without a profile, and an implementation MUST NOT enable it unless the application asks. A job that runs a program is not safe to replay. On a mailbox the ClientHello can sit for a day. The sentence should be: a ClientHello that carries early data is a malformed flight and is rejected. RFC 9147 Section 5.6 keeps epoch 1 and omits `EndOfEarlyData` because loss makes the deletion attack trivial. Epoch 1 is not a quiet place to put a job.

**Tickets.** Section 9.3 and Section 13 say SHOULD single-use, matching version 1 and matching RFC 8446 Section 8.1, which is a SHOULD, not a MUST. On this carriage the ticket mail sits in the same archive as the transcript. Make single-use mandatory for tickets issued here. Leave the UDP binding's SHOULD alone. The tests already cover a ticket used once, a corrupted ticket PSK, an expired ticket, a fresh share on resumption, and a `psk_ke`-only offer refused with `illegal_parameter`. The live run did not resume. The proof says that.

**`Re:` and `Fwd:`.** The tests accept one `Re:` prefix. The draft's ABNF does not. Specify at most one leading `Re:` or `Fwd:`, and only when the remainder is exactly a flight subject. Do not accept a chain of them.

**IMAP flags.** The draft never mentions them. RFC 9051 (which obsoletes RFC 3501): `BODY[section]` sets `\Seen`; `BODY.PEEK[section]` does not. The proof's third live attempt fetched RFC822, set `\Seen` on the client's own second ClientHello, and the runner's UNSEEN search never saw it. The working run used `BODY.PEEK[]`, bound mail to its own session-id, and marked seen only what it consumed. The first two attempts fed stale flights and poisoned the transcript; both failed closed. The rule belongs in Section 8 or Section 9: mark seen only flight mail that has been consumed; a fetch used to decide uses `BODY.PEEK`; an initiator ignores flight mail for a session-id it does not have open. DKIM (RFC 6376) and ARC (RFC 8617) authenticate a domain, not a job. They are the substitute gate Section 16.3 correctly forbids.

**Bounds the draft asserts and the code does not quite have.** `FlightReassembler.offer` sets `updated = now` on every accepted part, and `_expire` measures age from `updated`. The 3600 seconds is an idle timer. A drip more often than hourly holds a slot until the 64-transfer cap, and then the oldest is evicted. Add a deadline from the first part. Section 6 says the bound is count, size, and age. The age half is an idle timer, and the draft's "from its last part" is honest about the reference numbers and silent about the drip.

`_send_result_on_session` keys the hold by job ident alone (`self._job_session[ident] = cid`). Idents are chosen by the submitter and collide across submitters. The comment in that function says the file stays in the outbox for a fresh session. There is no age, count, or size bound. Goal 4 of the plan says delay does not create unbounded state. Expire the pending result. Log it. Do not send it. Dropping is not the downgrade Section 12 forbids. Downgrade would be sending it as ordinary mail. Hold the pending result under the connection id, or under `(key_id, job ident)`, not under the job ident alone.

The digest cache's 4096 is in the code (`_DigestCache`, `dedup_entries=4096`) and not in the draft. A FIFO of 4096 is an optimization against a poller delivering the same mail twice. It is not the replay defense. Section 13 already has the real defenses: the DTLS window, and the job seen-set. The `bytes=` field publishes the exact datagram length. Section 16.2 says the document specifies no padding, so the leak is consistent. If padding is ever added, it has to be the padded length, or the padding is undone by the header. One sentence next to the field is enough. Do not add padding now.

**Section 11.** The disclosure mechanism is version 1 Section 20, unchanged, and the latency argument is why it earns its place on a mailbox. The clause "for a superintelligent system driving the runner through email" does no protocol work. The requirement is "a submitter that plans work from the platform card." The disclosure goes out before the initiator's Finished, which is acceptable only because Section 9.1 step 4 has already checked the cookie and the PSK binder. Say that precondition in Section 11. A reader of Section 11 alone cannot tell whether an unauthenticated ClientHello is enough to extract a platform card. It must not be. `docs/project/future.md` still uses the same adjective in the version 1 future entry. That file is not this draft. The draft should not repeat it.

**Discovery.** Section 4.3 allows discovering the carriage by attempting a handshake. A failed attempt leaves ordinary email alone, and that sentence is right. A probe is several stored mails. An unauthenticated hint, trusted for nothing except whether to try, would avoid using a handshake as a probe. OPTIONAL, and not a gate.

**References and rendering.** The body cites RFC 5234 and RFC 4648. Neither is in the reference blocks. RFC 7748 should be added with X25519. RFC 9146 is in the normative block; it is the DTLS 1.2 connection-id document, and RFC 9147 is what this profile runs. If version 1 cites 9146 the same way, follow version 1 and say the connection id on this carriage is the one RFC 9147 uses. The version 1 normative reference is dated 2026-10 and names no revision. Pin the `-03`. RFC 9325 is already in the informative block, which is the right place for it. There is no `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v3-00.txt`, no `.xml`, and no idnits output. The header comment says how to render it. Do that before any submission. Rendering does not fix the six items above.

**The proof and the plan are stale in specific sentences.** The proof still says `dtls_over_email` at the lines that describe the build and the live run (the option in the code, the draft, the plan, and the changelog is `dtls_via_email`). A reader of both will think those are two switches. The plan still says each step lands on the `together` branch and nothing is pushed. The work was pushed, reviewed, merged at `4a61c5ba`, and released in 3.15.125. That sentence is now false.

### What I would change, in order, and what I would not touch

1. In the abstract and in Section 10, state that job objects stay version 1 objects.
2. Rewrite Section 9.2 to the address `_addr_for` actually uses, and state that the PSK identity selects the key.
3. Replace the naked `text/plain` body with a dedicated MIME part before this carriage is pointed at a mailbox the deployment does not control.
4. Delete "carrying the same datagram." Specify handshake-hit behavior as re-posting the saved last flight, and application-hit behavior as drop. Put the connection-id-before-digest rule into Section 7.2. Give the timer a floor in poll periods, which is the profile RFC 9147 Section 5.8.2 already allows.
5. Add the X25519 harvest limit, the RFC 7748 citation, and a named RFC 10024 slot to Section 16.1. Cut "removes the trade." Reduce the "2 ways" paragraph to the protocol fact.
6. Write the session-id alphabet, the 0-RTT rejection, mandatory single-use tickets on this carriage, the one-prefix `Re:`/`Fwd:` rule, and the IMAP `BODY.PEEK` rule.
7. Add the absolute reassembly deadline and the pending-result expiry. Name the 4096 digest cache as an optimization.
8. Pin the version 1 revision, add the missing references, fix `dtls_over_email` in the proof, fix the `together` sentence in the plan, render the draft, and run idnits.

I would not merge a further protocol change on top of this until that text exists, and I would not submit it. I would not reopen the profile, the gate, the fail-closed lane rule, the decision to reuse `dtlsproto.py`, or the decision that subjects do not authenticate. Those are the parts of the merged tree that are already doing the job.

## 2-Nov-5:38pm-PST/PDT — Grok: version 3, the review

Read of branch `homing-pigeon-v3` at `4cc16fe0`, posted at the conductor's word. The branch sits on `e2d255b7` (3.15.119). Main is `42871d7a` (3.15.124). Nothing here was rebased, merged, or submitted.

There is almost nothing left to build. The plan's definition of done is met in the tree: the specification, the future entry, the carriage, 45 tests, opt-in and default off. Two holes are documentary. The proof note still says resumption over mail is unproven, and the commit after it (`4cc16fe0`) added the resumption and provider-shape tests. And nothing on the branch has been run against current main.

Boilerplate that is agreed and has no research note (the RFC 2119 citation, the reference-block mechanics, the local-draft status repeated three times) is marked once and then skipped. Everything that carries a design claim is judged.

### Plan

The seed line and the SS answer. Agree. SS is the existing version 1 PSK, not a second secret. Folding that into the opening instead of leaving it only in the addendum is the right edit. No new key material is the correct constraint. Research: say in one sentence, at the moment the acronym is expanded, that the PSK is an authenticator only. "Shared secret" is the phrase that will be misread as the session key.

Version 1 profile as the cryptographic basis (DTLS 1.3 only, one suite, `psk_dhe_ke` only, X25519 on every handshake including resumption, empty `platform_info` in ClientHello, disclosure only in EncryptedExtensions, jobs still hit the acceptance gate). Agree with all nine bullets. This is the part of the design that should not be reopened. Research, and it is the one that matters: X25519 is classical. The threat model is that mail is stored forever. A later break of X25519 decrypts the recorded flights without ever learning the PSK. Forward secrecy against theft of the long-term secret is not forward secrecy against harvest-now-decrypt-later of the ephemeral. Do not block this build on it. Do write, in the security considerations, that the ephemeral is X25519, and leave a named slot for a hybrid share (X25519 + ML-KEM) under the same `psk_dhe_ke` rule: the PSK authenticates, the hybrid share encrypts. That is the upgrade this carriage will actually need, because the transcript is the thing being archived.

"The missing piece is carriage." Agree. Changing the datagram path and not the handshake is how the review surface stays one surface.

Goal 1, the thirteen spec contents. Agree with the list. Item 10 (no downgrade to `psk_ke`, cleartext, or per-job crypto for that session) is the rule the rest of the design is protecting. Item 12 (no IANA action yet) is correct for an unsubmitted -00.

Goal 2, expanding the one-line future entry. Agree, and the expanded entry does keep the owner's bold title.

Goal 3, production path. Agree with the insertion point: classify in the email extension, unwrap application data back into an ordinary envelope, deliver through `handle_mail`, return the result inside the session. Agree that the core must not grow a second job parser. Research: the sentence "the core cannot tell that the envelope arrived inside a session" is only safe if the email process, not the core, remembers the binding, and if that binding is keyed by more than a client-chosen job id. Job ids collide across submitters. Key the hold by `(key_id, job ident)` or by the session connection id plus the job ident. The draft's Section 10.3 says "identified for that session by its job," which is the weak form.

Goal 4, the session is a second gate. Agree with every bullet, including "a completed handshake does not authorize a job" and "session establishment does not create a new cleartext allowance." The duplicate layering (mail digest, DTLS record layer, job seen-set) is the right split. Research on the last bullet: "store-and-forward delay does not create unbounded reassembly" is claimed and then not quite implemented. The reassembler resets its 3600-second timer on every part (`updated = now` in `FlightReassembler.offer`). A slow drip holds a slot indefinitely, up to the 64-transfer cap. Add an absolute deadline from the first part, no longer than the idle timeout, and evict on that even if parts keep arriving.

Goal 5, the test list. Agree, and the file now covers it, including the resumption and loss cases the proof note has not caught up with. The fuzz doctrine matches the project's existing rule. Agree with "add no skips."

Goal 6 and the proof note. Agree with the rule that the note may record only unfiltered output. Disagree with the note as it sits on the branch tip: it describes 26 tests and lists resumption as unproven, after a later commit added 19 tests and says 469 touched tests passed. That note is now a false record. Updating it is the one completion worth doing on this branch before anything else.

D1, reuse `dtlsproto.py`. Agree. A second DTLS stack would be the wrong risk.

D2, subject routes, DTLS bytes authenticate. Agree. This is the sentence the whole carriage stands on.

D3, mailbox ownership stays in the email extension. Agree. A second poller on the same mailbox would race the seen-state.

D4, the core is the only job gate. Agree.

D5, opt-in, literal `true` only, secret handed over only then. Agree. Present-but-invalid stays off.

D6, synthetic peer address from the configured mailbox peer, not from `From`. Agree with the goal. Disagree with the sentence as a description of what was built, and the spec copies the same overclaim. The code is `_addr_for`: `addr = "email:" + (session_id or "legacy")`. The address is the mail session-id from the subject. There is no configured peer identity in it. That is a reasonable demux key, and it does avoid `From`. It is not what D6 says. Either change D6 and Section 9.2 to say "the address is `email:` plus the mail session-id, which is an untrusted routing label and never a credential," or actually mix in a configured peer id. The honest sentence is the better one. Binding the cookie to an attacker-chosen label is fine only while the cookie key stays a server secret. Say that, so nobody later derives the cookie from the session-id alone.

D7, segment at the carriage, reassemble before the engine. Agree. DTLS should keep seeing datagrams. Cutting on base64 group boundaries is the right detail. The limits (262144 octets, 32768-character chunk, 1024 parts, 64 transfers) are sane.

D8, exact-duplicate suppression before the engine. Agree for application data. Disagree with the combination of this rule and the retransmission sentence in the spec (a re-send "carries the same datagram" and a held digest is dropped before the stack). If both are true, a bit-identical retransmission never wakes the peer, so a lost response is repaired only by the local timer. The tests admit the engine dodges this by advancing the record sequence number, so the retransmit is not bit-identical and the cache does not eat it. That is an accident of the engine, not a rule. Research: on a digest hit for a handshake datagram, do not feed it back into key derivation; re-post the saved last outbound flight. On a digest hit for application data, drop it and do nothing. Then a future engine that retransmits identical bytes still makes progress, and a replayed job record still cannot re-enter the core.

D9, results stay in the session, no downgrade. Agree with the prohibition. Disagree with the unbounded wait. "If the session is gone, the result remains pending for a fresh session" has no age, count, or size bound, which contradicts Goal 4. A peer that opens a session, submits jobs, and vanishes fills the outbox forever. Expire the pending result, log it, and still do not send it as ordinary mail. Dropping is not downgrade.

Open question 2, resumption allowed. Agree, given `psk_dhe_ke` and a fresh share. The new tests (fresh share, single-use ticket, corrupted PSK, expired ticket, `psk_ke`-only refused, pipe round trip) are the right set. Research: single-use SHOULD is too soft for a mailbox. A stored ticket is a second long-lived secret sitting in the same archive this carriage is trying to make useless. Make single-use mandatory on this carriage, and state that a replayed ticket can open at most a handshake that still needs a fresh share.

Open question 3, size ceiling. Agree with the numbers as defaults. The ceiling is not the problem a provider will hit. Footers are. That is under the wire format.

"Each step lands on the `together` branch. Nothing is pushed." That sentence is now false as a status. The work is on `homing-pigeon-v3` and it is on the remote. The plan should say so or drop the sentence. Agree with "not submitted."

### Draft, front matter and abstract

Title, category info, independent submission, trust200902, unsubmitted. Agree. Do not submit this -00 as it stands. The two holes below (the wire format versus real MTAs, and the classical ephemeral) should be in the text first.

"Version 3 changes exactly one thing — the carriage." Agree, and the rest of the draft mostly keeps that promise. The places it does not are operational rules (timers, poll rhythm, logging), which are fine as long as they are labeled local.

"Version 3 removes the trade." Disagree with the slogan. It removes the confidentiality trade. It takes on latency, a stored transcript at every hop, and a pre-authentication surface the size of the mailbox. The introduction's next paragraphs actually say this, and they are better than the slogan. Keep those. Cut "removes the trade."

Email has no forward secrecy because the shared secret is long-lived and mail is stored. DTLS has forward secrecy and needs a live path. Agree with both. This is the best statement of the problem on the branch.

The four bullets (flights as mail, parser never routes a flight to the job path, jobs still face the gate, disclosure in EncryptedExtensions so no extra poll). Agree with all four. The disclosure bullet is the one most agreed with, for the latency reason.

### Terminology and profile

flight mail, mail session-id, transfer, carriage. Agree. Especially "not a credential, not a DTLS identity." The session-id regex in the code is `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,128}` excluding `.` and `..`, which is tighter than a vague `word-char` and is the right alphabet. No slashes, no whitespace. Put that alphabet in the ABNF instead of deferring to version 1's `word-char`, so a future widening of `word-char` does not put a newline into a session-id.

Profile unchanged, one suite, PSK is the version 1 shared secret, no second secret. Agree.

`psk_ke` MUST NOT be offered, accepted, or fallen back to. Fresh X25519 on full and resumed handshakes. Agree. This is the property. The test that a `psk_ke`-only offer dies with `illegal_parameter` is the right proof.

Stateless until the cookie validates. Connection IDs both ways. No 0-RTT. Agree, and the 0-RTT ban should be repeated in the security considerations as a MUST reject, not as "we do not use it." Early data on a store-and-forward path is a replay. A ClientHello that carries early data should be a failed flight, not a hello with the early data skipped.

No UDP port. Addresses are mailboxes. Agree.

"An implementation MUST NOT use this carriage as a reason to alter the cryptography, the handshake, the record protection, or the gate." Agree.

Opt-in. The secret crosses into the email process only when enabled. Agree. Same boundary as enabling the datagram listener.

"A submitter discovers the carriage by attempting it, or from a prior platform card. A failed attempt does not affect ordinary email." Agree that failure must not disable ordinary mail. Disagree that probing is a good discovery method. A probe is a handshake, which is several stored mails and a poll cycle, and it is indistinguishable from an attack. Research: an unauthenticated capability hint on the platform card (`dtls_over_email` as a fact, not a credential), so the first contact does not have to be a probe. An unauthenticated hint must not be trusted for admission. It only answers whether it is worth trying.

### Wire format

Subject `[HOMING-PIGEON] dtls <session-id>`. Subjects route, they never authenticate. MUST NOT treat `From`, `Reply-To`, `Message-ID`, the mailbox UID, or arrival order as identity. Agree with every one of those prohibitions.

The legacy unaddressed harness shape MAY be accepted for tests and MUST NOT be required by a deployment. Agree. The proof's last bullet is right to keep it off the deployment surface.

One header line, `HP-DTLS/3`, then one base64 chunk. The digest is SHA-384 of the whole datagram, not a signature. Agree with the grammar and with "MUST NOT treat the digest as authentication." It only answers whether these parts are one datagram.

"Unlike a job body, a flight body has no preamble and no trailer." Agree as a fail-closed rule. Disagree as a mail design. This is the operational hole. The new tests already show a provider footer and a subject suffix both die as `dtls-invalid`. A disclaimer, a gateway banner, or a list footer destroys the flight, and Section 8.3 then marks it seen so it is not retried. Research, and this should land before anyone points the carriage at a real provider: stop using a naked `text/plain` body as the frame. Carry the datagram as a MIME part with a private content type and `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64`. Ignore every other part, including `text/plain` footers. Keep the header line inside that part. The parser already walks MIME for the first `text/plain`; point it at a typed part instead. Line wrapping inside base64 can stay tolerated. Quoted-printable must not. No IANA request is needed while the draft is local.

`Re:` still routes, in the tests, and the spec never mentions it. Disagree by omission. A reply thread will mix flights if any prefix is stripped casually. Research: accept at most one leading `Re:` or `Fwd:` and only when the remainder matches the flight subject exactly. Document it. Do not accept an arbitrary prefix.

Size cap before base64 decode, before reassembly, before the stack. Agree. This is the right order and it is the sentence that makes the pre-authentication bound real.

Reference limits 262144 / 32768 / 1024. Agree as local defaults. A sender that does not know the peer SHOULD stay inside them. Agree.

### Reassembly, retransmission, duplicates

Parts in any order. Duplicate parts idempotent. A contradiction destroys the transfer. Complete once, then discard. Expire. Agree with all five. The contradiction rule (drop, do not arbitrate) is correct because the digest is unauthenticated: there is no honest way to pick a winner.

"Reassembly is unauthenticated and therefore cheap to discard." Agree. Best one-sentence threat model in the reassembly section.

"The carriage defines no acknowledgement and no retransmission timer of its own." Agree. Two retransmission machines on one path multiply mail.

"A re-send is a new transfer id carrying the same datagram." Disagree, for the reason under D8. Same bytes are a duplicate and should be suppressed. A retransmission the peer must react to has to be either byte-different or handled as "digest hit, resend last flight, do not reprocess." The spec should pick one. The implementation has quietly picked "byte-different because the sequence number moved," and the spec does not say that.

"SHOULD stretch retransmission timers toward the poll rhythm. Stretching changes no message." Agree that it changes no message. Research: state a floor, or an hourly poller will give up on a live-path timer before the next poll. Interval at least two poll periods, give up after eight, and the give-up is the Section 9.4 deadline. The "about 4 poll cycles" sentence later in the draft is the right mental model; tie the timer to it.

Three duplicate layers, each doing only its job. A duplicate flight MUST NOT create a second session, MUST NOT deliver application data twice, MUST NOT run a job twice. Agree. The implementation detail agreed with: the connection-id binding check runs before the digest cache, so a mislabeled copy cannot poison the cache against the legitimate copy. That comment in `_receive_datagram` is the right order. The spec should steal that sentence.

No ordering assumed. Job-mail arrival order is not an input. Agree.

### Parser and fail-closed

Classify before delivery. A flight subject goes only to the carriage. A job subject goes to the gate. Everything else stays other mail. Agree.

Initialization is recognized from the bytes: the first record is a plaintext ClientHello, including a fragmented one. Agree. Recognizing it from the subject would be the bug D2 forbids.

Malformed flight mail is marked seen, dropped, and never reread as a job. Agree, with the MIME caveat above. Fail-closed plus "mark seen" is correct against poll loops and brutal against a gateway that appends a footer. Once the frame lives in its own MIME part, a footer in `text/plain` stops being malformed and this rule goes back to catching actual garbage.

"The lane a message enters by its subject is the only lane it is ever read in." Agree. Do not soften this.

### Session establishment

The five-step handshake, and none of it is a job. Agree. Stateless HelloRetryRequest before session state. Agree.

Section 9.2, the synthetic address. Disagree with the sentence as written. It says the address is derived from the configured mailbox peer and from the mail session-id "and from nothing the message itself asserts." The mail session-id is asserted by the message. The sentence contradicts itself, and the code uses only the session-id. Rewrite it to the code, as under D6. The following bullets are agreed: a protected record is accepted under a mail session-id only when its destination connection id is the one bound to that session-id, and a connection id bound to a different session-id is dropped whichever subject it arrived under. That binding is what makes relabeling useless. Entry is the record protection. Agree.

Resumption: new mail session-id, fresh share, `psk_dhe_ke` only, disclosure again. MAY disable by configuration. Agree. See the single-use note above.

A failed attempt expires and does not touch ordinary email or any other attempt. Agree.

MUST NOT keep keys or the session-id binding past the configured lifetime. A result for a dead session is not sent on any other path. Agree with both. Add the expiry-and-drop bound from D9 so "not sent on any other path" does not become "kept forever."

### Jobs, results, disclosure

A 4-octet length prefix, one envelope per application message, subject, blank line, body. Agree. Reusing the channel envelope is what lets the core stay ignorant.

Handshake completion MUST NOT bypass the gate. HMAC, audience, size caps, staleness, key policy, all unchanged. The session authenticates the channel; the signature authenticates the object. Agree. This is the other sentence that should not be edited out. The cleartext rule surviving the session is correct: the session's confidentiality is not the object's provenance.

A result is sealed into that session. MUST NOT ride a different job's session. Agree. Cross-session result injection would be worse than downgrade.

The disclosure mechanism is unchanged from version 1 Section 20. Agree.

The latency argument, including the "superintelligent system" clause. Agree with the argument: on a mailbox, a post-handshake greeting costs a poll cycle at best and a day at worst, so the disclosure belongs on the flight the handshake already sends. Disagree with the adjective. It does no technical work. "A submitter that plans work from the platform card" says the same thing.

The cost: the disclosure goes out before the initiator's Finished is verified. Adopt version 1's argument. Agree, on the condition that the PSK binder has already been checked, which the flight list says it has. An aborting peer who holds the PSK learning the disclosure is the same as a real peer learning it. A peer who does not hold the PSK must not get it. The spec should say that precondition in Section 11, not only by reference to the flight order.

### No-downgrade, replay, operations

The six no-downgrade bullets. Agree with all six. The one to put first is the last: mailbox TLS is not a substitute for the session, and "the IMAP connection was TLS" is not a reason to run a session-less job with session-level trust. That is the downgrade an operator will actually propose.

Replay at three layers, no third execution window. Agree. The two priced windows from version 1 stay the only ones. This carriage adds no execution path, so it adds no replay path to execution. That sentence is true only while application data is forced through `handle_mail`. Any future shortcut that runs a job from the DTLS callback breaks it. The closing sentence of Section 16.6 already says so.

A replayed ticket restarts a handshake that still needs fresh ephemeral keys. Agree. Another reason single-use should be MUST on this carriage.

Flight mail and job mail share a mailbox and neither delays the other beyond one poll pass. Disabling the option returns the mailbox to its previous behavior. Agree.

Poll rhythm is the operational dial. Twenty seconds is about four polls; hourly is about four hours. Agree. This is the right operator sentence. Pair it with the timer floor.

SHOULD log establishment, abandonment, expiry, and malformed drops. MUST NOT log keys, ephemeral material, or the shared secret. Agree.

### IANA and security considerations

No IANA action. Reuse version 1's `platform_info` code point. Do not request a second extension, a port, a media type, or an OID. Agree. If the MIME type from the wire-format note is ever standardized, that is a later document's request, not this one's.

Section 16.1, the forward-secrecy claim. Agree with the claim as scoped: record the flights, later steal the PSK, the records stay confidential, because the ephemeral private keys never left the endpoints and do not survive the session. Agree with the limits: PSK theft still impersonates and still intercepts future handshakes, still reads ordinary version 1 job crypto, and does nothing about an endpoint that is compromised while the session is live. "No more, and no less" is the right ending. The missing limit is the one in the plan note: a later break of X25519, not of the PSK, defeats recorded sessions. Write it next to the other limits so the property does not get cited as stronger than it is.

The mailbox holds the transcript in the clear at the record layer. It cannot read application data, forge a verifying record, or finish a handshake. It can delay, drop, duplicate, and reorder. No padding, no cover traffic. Agree with all of that. Agree that metadata (session-id, cadence, sizes) is observable and that the document should keep saying it specifies no padding. Do not add cover traffic in this version. Research, only if a deployment cares: pad each datagram to a fixed set of buckets before base64, and keep the real length inside the protected record, not in the `bytes=` header. The `bytes=` field currently publishes the exact length. That is consistent with "we specify no padding." If padding is ever added, that field has to become the padded length or it undoes the padding.

Headers are not authentication. A flight is worth exactly what its datagram proves. Agree.

"A deployment MUST NOT add a sender allowlist as a substitute gate." Agree if "substitute" is the operative word. Disagree if it is read as "you may not filter mail." An allowlist, or a DKIM or ARC check, is a reasonable pre-filter and a terrible gate. The version 1 property that email has no sender gate should stay. Research: allow a documented, untrusted intake filter that can only drop, never admit, and say in the same sentence that passing the filter grants nothing the datagram did not already prove.

Pre-authentication bounds: size cap, bounded reassembly, bounded digest cache, stateless cookie, malformed mail consumed once, disclosure tier is the responder's choice. Agree with the list. The drip-timer hole above is the one bound that is not actually a bound. The digest cache is a 4096-entry FIFO in the code; the spec only says "bounded." Name the bound or say the bound is local. FIFO eviction means a sender of 4096 distinct junk datagrams can push a still-useful digest out and then replay it. For application data the job seen-set still holds. For handshakes, a flushed digest can re-enter the stack. That is acceptable only because the stack is supposed to be safe against replay on its own. Say that, so the cache is understood as an optimization against poller double-delivery, not as the replay defense.

"At no point does this carriage answer a failure by doing the same thing more weakly." Agree. This is the downgrade policy in one sentence. The examples that follow (plain job crypto, an ordinary mail result, reading a flight as a job) are the three to keep as the test oracles, and the tests already cover them.

Section 16.6, the gate is the execution boundary. Several holders of the shared secret are distinguished by additional secrets and key policy, not by the session. Forward secrecy changes transport, not admission. A future extension that admits a job on the session's authority alone is a proposal to remove the gate. Agree with the whole paragraph. It is the right last word.

One gap inside that paragraph: the synthetic peer is singular ("the peer the runner is configured to serve"), while this section admits several key holders. The PSK identity inside the handshake has to be what selects `additional_secrets`. The mail session-id must not select the key. The code's `secret_lookup` callback is the right shape; the spec should say the selection is the DTLS PSK identity, so nobody maps session-id to key as a shortcut.

### What to do next, and what not to do

Do not merge this onto main yet, and do not submit it. The carriage design is sound. The profile reuse is sound. The gate split is sound. The tests are aimed at the right failures.

In order:

1. Rewrite Section 9.2 and plan D6 so they describe the address the code uses.
2. Split duplicate handling into "a handshake hit resends the last flight" versus "an application hit is dropped," and stop claiming the retransmit is the same datagram.
3. Put an absolute reassembly deadline next to the idle timer, and a pending-result expiry next to the no-downgrade rule.
4. Move the frame into its own MIME part before anyone points this at a real provider. The footer test is already saying the current body will not survive.
5. Add the X25519 harvest limit, and a named hybrid-share slot, to Section 16.1.
6. Update the proof note so it stops denying the resumption tests, then rebase onto current main and run the touched suites there.

The architecture is agreed. Four sentences overclaim what the code does or what the bounds are, and the wire format depends on an untouched `text/plain` body. The research that changes the design, rather than the wording, is the hybrid ephemeral and the MIME part.


